# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Docker build context — DENY EVERYTHING, then allow the two files the image
# actually needs.
#
# WHY A WHITELIST. The build context moved to the repository root so the image
# can read scraper/requirements.txt as the ONE source of truth for the discovery
# engine's dependencies. The root is ~19 GB (frontend/.next alone is 17 GB) and
# contains both .env files, so a deny-list here would be one forgotten pattern
# away from shipping a 19 GB context or baking a credential into an image.
#
# Starting from `*` inverts the risk: anything new added to the repo is excluded
# by default, and including it has to be a deliberate edit to this file.
#
# Verify after changing this file — never assume:
#   docker compose build worker        # read "transferring context: …"
#   docker run --rm viteseo-php:8.4 find / -maxdepth 3 -name ".env*"
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# 1. Exclude everything.
*

# 2. Allow only what the Dockerfile reads.
#    - the Dockerfile itself (compose reads it from the context)
#    - the scraper's dependency manifest, baked into /opt/scraper-venv
!docker/php/Dockerfile
!scraper/requirements.txt

# Nothing else is needed: the application source is bind-mounted at runtime
# (./backend -> /app, ./scraper -> /scraper:ro), never copied into the image.
#
# Consequently these are all excluded by rule 1 above, and are listed here only
# so the intent is searchable — do NOT re-include any of them:
#   .git/                  history, remotes, credentials in URLs
#   **/.env, **/.env.*     backend/.env, frontend/.env.local, scraper/.env
#   frontend/.next/        17 GB of build output
#   **/node_modules/       881 MB
#   backend/vendor/        351 MB
#   scraper/.venv/         124 MB, built for the HOST interpreter
#   backend/storage/       logs, uploads, local lead attachments
#   docs/, *.md            not needed to build a runtime image
